<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?><rss xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom" version="2.0"><channel><title>World News, Today World News, Latest International News, World Breaking News, Trending News of World - Times of India</title><link>https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world</link><description>World News Today: Read the latest world news headlines, Current International breaking news world wide. In depth analysis and top news headlines world wide.</description><language>en-gb</language><lastBuildDate>2026-08-26T19:26:03+05:30</lastBuildDate><atom:link type="application/rss+xml" rel="self" href="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/rssfeeds/296589292.cms"/><copyright>Copyright:(C) Wed, 26 Aug 2026 19:26:03 IST Bennett Coleman &amp; Co. Ltd, http://in.indiatimes.com/policyterms/1554651.cms</copyright><docs>http://syndication.indiatimes.com/</docs><image><title>World News, Today World News, Latest International News, World Breaking News, Trending News of World - Times of India</title><url>https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/507610.cms</url><link>https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world</link></image><item><title><![CDATA[Singapore is paying parents $55,000 to have babies; here's why]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133538724.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[In a groundbreaking move, Singapore is launching a substantial financial support package designed for families with children. Targeted at reversing the alarming trend of declining birth rates, the government will provide up to S$70,000 per child until they reach seventeen. This initiative replaces previous schemes, providing consistent financial relief to parents, thereby encouraging family growth in the nation.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/rest-of-world/singapore-will-pay-about-55000-in-support-for-each-child-why-the-country-is-paying-parents-to-have-babies/articleshow/133538724.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/rest-of-world/singapore-will-pay-about-55000-in-support-for-each-child-why-the-country-is-paying-parents-to-have-babies/articleshow/133538724.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-08-26T16:41:48+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI Lifestyle Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133538935,imgsize-1057675.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Michigan puts $6.5 million toward 22,650 Keweenaw acres as conservation fund tops $1.4 billion]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133538900.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[Michigan’s Natural Resources Trust Fund is marking 50 years of supporting land conservation and outdoor recreation. Created in 1976 using earnings from state-owned oil, gas and mineral revenues, the fund has awarded nearly $1.4 billion so far. Its latest grants include $6.5 million to acquire nearly 22,650 acres in the Keweenaw Peninsula, along with funding for parks, trails, waterfront areas and recreation projects across Michigan.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/in-1976-michigan-created-a-conservation-fund-from-oil-gas-and-mineral-revenues-50-years-later-it-has-awarded-nearly-1-4-billion-and-is-putting-6-5-million-toward-almost-22650-keweenaw-acres/articleshow/133538900.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/in-1976-michigan-created-a-conservation-fund-from-oil-gas-and-mineral-revenues-50-years-later-it-has-awarded-nearly-1-4-billion-and-is-putting-6-5-million-toward-almost-22650-keweenaw-acres/articleshow/133538900.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-08-26T18:14:12+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI World Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133539635,imgsize-140737.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[A New Zealand sailor spent years living alone on a remote Cook Islands atoll across 3 separate stays]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133539848.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[Suwarrow atoll, once the secluded refuge of hermit Tom Neale, has transformed into the Cook Islands' inaugural conservation sanctuary. After Neale spent sixteen years in seclusion on Anchorage Island, the atoll was designated as a national park and bird haven. This vital area now protects critical seabird nesting grounds and marine ecosystems, allowing Neale's legacy to be honored through his preserved hut and a tribute on the island.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/rest-of-world/a-new-zealand-sailor-spent-years-living-alone-on-a-remote-cook-islands-atoll-across-three-separate-stays-a-year-after-his-final-stay-ended-suwarrow-was-declared-a-national-park/articleshow/133539848.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/rest-of-world/a-new-zealand-sailor-spent-years-living-alone-on-a-remote-cook-islands-atoll-across-three-separate-stays-a-year-after-his-final-stay-ended-suwarrow-was-declared-a-national-park/articleshow/133539848.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-08-26T17:21:13+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI World Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133540062,imgsize-1119008.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[3 Telangana students killed in Florida road accident, one identified as Nizamabad native]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133542639.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/3-tela